@name Explainer 9: Clark-type symmetric Gauss 50mm f/4
# Explainer #9: four-element symmetric air-spaced Gauss type (Alvan Clark
# 1888): on each side of the stop a positive N-SK16 meniscus outside and a
# negative F2 meniscus inside, all concave towards the stop.
# Role: the triplet's negative lens split around the stop; symmetry is back
# and oblique rays meet the surfaces nearly head-on.
# Design: the front half's curvatures, thicknesses and spacings vary,
# mirrored to the rear half (EFL 50 mm, stop gaps >= 3 mm), minimising the
# white-light RMS spot at 0, 0.5, 0.7 and 1.0 of the field, object at 2.5 m;
# vignetting towards the corner up to half the light. Fastest useful
# f-number: none of f/2.8-f/4 is sharp; f/4 shown. Start: an earlier run
# of this job from a rough sketch.
#
# Generated by `lens-render optimize lenses/explainer/09-clark.toml`.
# RMS spot (F, e, C) at f/4, object at 2.5 m, by image height
# (fraction of 21.63 mm): 0: 16.6, 0.5: 36.4, 0.7: 33.0, 1: 30.5 µm. Units: mm.
#
# radius      thickness   material       semi-diameter
